 six weeks old and Arizona leans R+2, keeping uncertainty elevated"], "rationale": "Every poll of the race, including Republican-sponsored ones, shows Hobbs ahead by one to fifteen points, and markets price her win probability near ninety percent. Her large fundraising edge and incumbency offset Arizona's Republican lean, though the stale polling and a Lean D consensus among raters argue against a wider edge.", "watch": "Fresh late-October polling or a late-breaking development, such as the 'predator protector' accusations or a split among Mormon women voters, could tighten or widen the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Katie Hobbs (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Arizona's governor's race pits incumbent Democrat Katie Hobbs against Republican Andy Biggs, with minor-party candidates also on the ballot. Every published poll since spring shows Hobbs ahead, with margins ranging from one to fifteen points and an average near eight. Republican-sponsored pollsters report narrower leads than independent firms, but none shows Biggs in front. Betting markets on Kalshi, Polymarket and PredictIt put Hobbs's chances near ninety percent, and most race raters call the contest Lean D, with one rating it Likely D.\n\nHobbs has raised roughly three times as much money as Biggs, about ten million dollars to his three point two million, though both held one to two million in cash as fall began. Biggs trails in both polls and market pricing, and headlines describe him as a long shot, while coverage highlights a potential split among Mormon women voters and accusations casting a shadow over the race. The newest poll dates to mid-August, leaving room for movement in the final weeks.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9144, "margin": 7.07}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9047, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9201, "margin": 7.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9198, "margin": 7.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8839, "margin": 7.